WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:03.670
Rhiannon: Children’s human rights
are the basic things, that children

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:07.370
need to thrive, they need to be
free and live with dignity and be

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:09.310
protected from violence or abuse.

00:00:09.849 --> 00:00:13.560
They should have an adequate standard
of living, the right to education, to

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.040
play, be healthy, and be cared for.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:24.140
Leah: Children need to have their own
rights over and above ‘human rights’

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:28.490
related to adults, because they are
separate and unique to adults, and need

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:32.700
special protection because of their
vulnerability and developing maturity.

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:36.830
They are often less articulate than
adults and have special developmental

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:41.270
needs and evolving capacities and are
less likely to be taken seriously.

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:46.000
Rhiannon: Once a child or young person
develops mental health concerns those

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.470
human rights can become more of an
issue, but they are no different.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.319
Children and young people are entitled
to respect for their privacy and

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:57.500
confidentiality, for example, in
getting advice and counselling on

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.920
mental health matters, depending
on their age and understanding.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.209
Leah: Many of the aims of services
helping children and young people

00:01:05.219 --> 00:01:08.839
who are experiencing mental health,
emotional and psychological issues,

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.189
align with human rights values.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.530
These include supporting young
people to be involved in their

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:15.710
care and treatment plans.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.360
Children and young people should be
treated with dignity and respect in their

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:25.280
interactions with services, and it should
be ensured that family members or carers

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.319
are able to participate in discussions.

00:01:28.039 --> 00:01:31.020
Rhiannon: All children with mental
health issues need to be protected

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.610
from violence, abuse, stigma and
mistreatment in institutions such as

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:39.910
health and social care facilities,
schools and the criminal justice system.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.060
Any ‘seclusion’ or ‘isolation’ of a
young person against their will or

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:52.280
without parental consent, especially
where the young person lacks competence

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:56.470
or capacity to consent, could lead
to potential human rights issues.

00:01:57.059 --> 00:02:01.410
Leah: Seclusion or isolation should only
really be used as a last resort, and

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:05.529
not as a punishment or way to encourage
good behaviour, as this could have a

00:02:05.529 --> 00:02:07.460
serious impact on the young person.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:12.049
Children’s best interests should
always be central, taking action

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.610
to enable all children and young
people to have the best outcomes.

